<p>
  Knight anoles arrived from Cuba in 1952, first turning up on the University of
  Miami campus in Coral Gables, and are now well established across much of south
  Florida, especially in leafy suburbs. They are the largest anoles in Florida:
  males reach about 19 cm from snout to vent, before you count the tail.
</p>
<p>
  They spend most of their time high in the canopy and are hard to spot. On hot
  summer days they come lower, and at Fairchild Tropical Botanic Garden you will
  often find them two or three meters up, facing head-down on the trunks of royal
  palms.
</p>

<h2>An anole that eats fruit, lizards and snakes</h2>
<p>
  Insects make up most of a knight anole's diet, yet fruit, especially figs and
  palm fruit, can make up over 80% of the stomach contents of some individuals. In
  2017 we found knight anoles with diets dominated by the fruit of native trees,
  including the endangered royal palm, and showed that seeds passed through their
  gut are still viable. It was the first evidence of any anole dispersing viable
  seeds, raising the possibility that an introduced lizard is now planting native
  trees.
</p>
<p>
  They are also predators of other lizards. We have recorded knight anoles eating
  bark anoles, and in November 2024 at Fairchild we caught a sleeping adult male,
  15 cm from snout to vent, that had just swallowed a red corn snake measuring
  32 cm from snout to vent. As far as we know it is the first documented case of a
  knight anole eating a snake in Florida. Knight anoles are normally active only
  by day, but we have also seen them active next to lights at night.
</p>
